@meta
id: 07-08
title: Drill — block, end, semicolon
chapter: 07
type: drill
skills: paragraph-motion, line-end
par: 46
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: six scroll-and-clicks, or a regex you will get wrong
open: fixtures.ts

@brief
Six fixture objects, none of them terminated. Put a semicolon on the closing
brace of each. The export list at the bottom is already correct.

Nothing to work out. Jump the block, step back one line, append, step down
onto the gap again. The dojo will hand you the file eight times.

@keys
| `}` | forward to the next blank line |
| `k` | up a line |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
`}` overshoots onto the blank line below the block, so `k` puts you on the
closing brace.
--
End the rep with `j` to step back onto the gap. From the brace itself the
next `}` would only move you one line; from the gap it clears a whole block.
Six keys per block, six blocks, and the rep never changes.

@solution
    } k A ; <Esc> j   } k A ; <Esc> j   } k A ; <Esc> j
    } k A ; <Esc> j   } k A ; <Esc> j   } k A ; <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Thirty-nine keystrokes for six edits spread over forty lines.

`}` then `k` is a pattern you will use for the rest of your life: the
paragraph motion is deliberately coarse, so you aim past the target and step
back one. The `j` that closes the rep is the other half of the same idea —
you put yourself back on the gap, because the gap is where `}` works from. The alternative — counting `6j`, `5j`, `6j` down the file — is
wrong as soon as one fixture gains a field, and this file is exactly the
kind that gains fields.

@start fixtures.ts
const orderPending = {
  id: 'ord_1042',
  customerId: 'cus_88',
  status: 'PENDING',
  amount: 4999,
}

const orderCompleted = {
  id: 'ord_1043',
  customerId: 'cus_91',
  status: 'COMPLETED',
  amount: 1250,
}

const orderCancelled = {
  id: 'ord_1044',
  customerId: 'cus_88',
  status: 'CANCELLED',
  amount: 899,
}

const orderRefunded = {
  id: 'ord_1045',
  customerId: 'cus_92',
  status: 'REFUNDED',
  amount: 15750,
}

const orderShipped = {
  id: 'ord_1046',
  customerId: 'cus_17',
  status: 'SHIPPED',
  amount: 2400,
}

const orderExpired = {
  id: 'ord_1047',
  customerId: 'cus_53',
  status: 'EXPIRED',
  amount: 640,
}

export const orderFixtures = [
  orderPending,
  orderCompleted,
  orderCancelled,
  orderRefunded,
  orderShipped,
  orderExpired,
];

